.TH MU2EDAQ-POWER-RECOVERY-BOOTSTRAP 1 "2026-09-18" "mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.1.0" "Mu2e DAQ Operations"
.SH NAME
bootstrap.sh, bootstrap.ps1, start-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh,
start-mu2edaq-power-recovery.ps1, stop-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh,
stop-mu2edaq-power-recovery.ps1 \- set up and control a recovery run
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./bootstrap.sh
.RB [ \-\-with\-cpp ]
.br
.B ./bootstrap.ps1
.RB [ \-WithCpp ]
.br
.B ./start-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh
.RI [ options ...]
.br
.B ./start-mu2edaq-power-recovery.ps1
.RI [ options ...]
.br
.B ./stop-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.RB [ \-\-status ]
.br
.B ./stop-mu2edaq-power-recovery.ps1
.RB [ \-Force ]
.RB [ \-Status ]
.RB [ \-Grace
.IR SECONDS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.SS bootstrap.sh, bootstrap.ps1
Creates or refreshes the project's Python virtual environment in
.IR venv/ ,
installs the package in editable mode with its dependencies, regenerates
.I requirements.txt
from what was actually resolved, and creates the
.IR logs/ ", " data/ ", " html/ " and " core/
directories.
.PP
It is idempotent and quick when there is nothing to do, which matters because
phase 0 of
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1)
calls it whenever a self-update touched
.IR pyproject.toml ,
.I requirements.txt
or the C++ sources.
.PP
.B \-\-with\-cpp
additionally configures and builds
.BR libmu2eprobe (3)
with CMake. That library is optional; without it the tools use a pure-Python
reachability fallback.
.PP
.B MU2EDAQ_PYTHON
selects the interpreter (default
.BR python3 );
Python 3.9 is the minimum.
.B VENV_DIR
relocates the environment.
.PP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
prints the script's header comment as usage and exits. The PowerShell form
takes
.B \-WithCpp
instead of
.BR \-\-with\-cpp ,
and documents itself through
.BR Get\-Help .
.SS start-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh, start-mu2edaq-power-recovery.ps1
Bootstraps the environment if it is missing, then passes every argument
straight to
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1).
This is the entry point to use from a fresh clone, because it works before the
venv exists.
.PP
With no arguments it runs
.B \-\-phase assess
\(em the read-only survey \(em on the principle that an accidental bare
invocation should look at the cluster rather than change it.
.PP
It writes a pid file to
.I logs/power-recovery.pid
and refuses to start a second concurrent run, which would drive the same
cluster from two directions and interleave power commands. A pid file left by a
process that is no longer running is removed rather than treated as a live run.
.B PID_FILE
relocates it in the shell version.
.PP
Neither script has options of its own: every argument is handed to
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1),
so
.B \-\-help
and
.B \-\-version
reach the driver rather than the wrapper. The two behave identically; the
PowerShell version differs only in calling
.I venv\eScripts\emu2e-power-recovery.exe
rather than
.IR venv/bin/mu2e-power-recovery .
.SS stop-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh
Stops a running recovery. SIGTERM first, then SIGKILL after a grace period
.RB ( GRACE ,
default 30 seconds), and only if it is needed. A pid file naming a process that
is no longer running is reported as stale and removed.
.PP
.B "Neither path is a clean stop."
The driver installs no SIGTERM handler, so the default disposition applies and
the process is terminated where it stands. It does not record the interruption,
the run is left in the store as
.I running
rather than
.IR interrupted ,
and the credential cleanup does not execute \(em so the caches the run minted,
including any root-capable service tickets, survive it. Check with
.B klist \-l
and destroy what is left by name with
.BR "kdestroy \-c" .
.PP
.BR SIGINT " is"
the clean stop: Ctrl-C at the terminal the run is on records the interruption,
marks the run
.IR interrupted ,
and runs cleanup. Prefer it when you have that terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Skip the grace period and kill at once. The run store does not record the
interruption \(em but, as above, it does not on the SIGTERM path either.
.TP
.B \-\-status
Report whether a run is active and change nothing.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the script's header comment as usage and exit.
.PP
Any other argument is rejected with exit status 2, rather than being passed on
to something that might act on it.
.SS stop-mu2edaq-power-recovery.ps1
The Windows equivalent, with the same two-stage behaviour: it asks the driver
to close, waits out the grace period, and only then terminates the process.
Its parameters follow PowerShell convention rather than the shell script's.
.TP
.B \-Force
Terminate immediately, without the grace period. The run store may not record
the interruption.
.TP
.B \-Status
Report whether a run is active and change nothing.
.TP
.BI \-Grace " SECONDS"
How long to wait before terminating. Default: 30. The shell script takes the
same value from the
.B GRACE
environment variable instead, which is the one difference between the two.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I venv/
The virtual environment.
.TP
.I logs/power-recovery.pid
Pid of the active run.
.TP
.I requirements.txt
Regenerated by
.BR bootstrap.sh .
Edit
.I pyproject.toml
instead.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.EX
\&./bootstrap.sh
\&./bootstrap.sh \-\-with\-cpp
MU2EDAQ_PYTHON=python3.9 ./bootstrap.sh

\&./start-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh                       # survey only
\&./start-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh \-\-phase all \-\-simulate
\&./stop-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh \-\-status
GRACE=90 ./stop-mu2edaq-power-recovery.sh
.EE
.RE
.PP
The same three steps on Windows:
.RS
.EX
\&.\ebootstrap.ps1 \-WithCpp
\&.\estart\-mu2edaq\-power\-recovery.ps1 \-\-phase all \-\-simulate
\&.\estop\-mu2edaq\-power\-recovery.ps1 \-Status
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1),
.BR mu2edaq-power-recovery (7)
